{"tool_name":"fractional-splines-wavelets","contents":["neubias"],"fetched_metadata":{"neubias":{"body":[{"format":"basic_html","processed":"<p>The fractional splines are an extension of the polynomial splines for all fractional degrees \u03b1 &gt; -1. Their basic constituents are piecewise power functions of degree \u03b1. One constructs the corresponding B-splines through a localization process similar to the classical one, replacing finite differences by fractional differences. The fractional B-splines share virtually all the properties of the classical B-splines, including the two-scale relation, and can therefore be used to define new wavelet bases with a continuously-varying order parameter</p>","summary":"","value":"<p>The fractional splines are an extension of the polynomial splines for all fractional degrees \u03b1 &gt; -1. Their basic constituents are piecewise power functions of degree \u03b1. One constructs the corresponding B-splines through a localization process similar to the classical one, replacing finite differences by fractional differences. The fractional B-splines share virtually all the properties of the classical B-splines, including the two-scale relation, and can therefore be used to define new wavelet bases with a continuously-varying order parameter</p>\r\n"}],"changed":[{"format":"Y-m-d\\TH:i:sP","value":"2019-10-16T17:02:00+00:00"}],"created":[{"format":"Y-m-d\\TH:i:sP","value":"2013-10-11T13:08:43+00:00"}],"default_langcode":[{"value":true}],"attribution_instructions":[],"comments_2023":[],"free_tagging":[{"target_id":4932,"target_type":"taxonomy_term","target_uuid":"4760fc2c-9590-45ff-b4eb-da764f474297","url":"/taxonomy/term/4932"},{"target_id":4844,"target_type":"taxonomy_term","target_uuid":"73ef6211-bd30-422c-bd73-5b4a6f4746f8","url":"/taxonomy/term/4844"}],"give_feedback_on_this_soft":[{"cid":0,"comment_count":0,"last_comment_name":null,"last_comment_timestamp":1505206739,"last_comment_uid":164,"status":2}],"has_author":[{"value":